// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// specs/t27os/chat_host.t27 -- where person to person chat touches the machine (owner, 2026-10-22; Refs
// gHashTag/t27#11560). Every decision (payload, dedup, order, ACK, retry, read, unread, the file format, idle close)
// is a tested fn of chat_core.t27; this file only moves bytes:
//   * the log of one friend: ~/.t27os/chat/<20 hex of the fingerprint>.t27 (chat_core cf_name / cf_encode), read
//     whole at start, written whole and atomically (peer_identity_host ih_write_atomic: .tmp with mode 0600, write,
//     fsync, rename; the directory made with mode 0700). A file that does not check is moved aside to <name>.bad
//     (kept, never written over) and the log starts empty. The log keeps the newest CL_KEEP (500) lines.
//   * the message session: one call_session block opened with CS_AUDIO_NONE on its own UDP port (the chat port; the
//     call port is not shared, so an INVITE that arrives here is a message session by construction). The same
//     handshake, the same 6-digit code and the same AES-128-GCM channel as a call, with no audio and no video.
//     chm_tick answers a RINGING session only when the caller is in the friends table and is the friend of this
//     log. A session from someone who is not a friend is left ringing for the window (CHM_E_STRANGER): the window
//     answers it to take an invitation (invites.t27) or declines it in the quiet hour after a DECLINE; the session
//     then ends by itself with CS_R_INVITE (call_session) and chm_tick says so once (CHM_E_INVITE, cs_id_proven
//     gives the fingerprint and the proven key). It never opens and none of its frames reaches a log. It never says "match" itself: no person compares a code in a message session,
//     so the session opens only when the friend proves the identity key stored for it (call_session cs_identity,
//     peer_identity pid_verdict TRUSTED); a friend whose key is not stored yet (never compared a code in a call, or
//     a v1 friends file) is refused with CS_R_UNVERIFIED and the messages wait. Behaviour, not an audit.
// A window drives one friend's chat with chm_init, chm_addr (the friend's address changed), chm_send, chm_open (the chat
// is shown: READ) and chm_tick from its main loop; chm_stat reads the counters. One log is open at a time: a session
// from another friend is left ringing and reported (CHM_E_OTHER, chm_ringing) so the window can open that friend's log
// and answer on its next tick; chat_probe.t27 drives it headless, glass_window.t27 from the cabinet (chat_view.t27).
module T27osChatHost;

